1
00:00:20,020 --> 00:00:43,028
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I wonder if we can discuss today what is feeling and how is feeling different from emotion and also from sensation, because these three are commonly mixed up or regarded as the same thing, whereas actually they're quite different.

2
00:00:43,889 --> 00:00:46,970
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I thought it would be good to clear that up.

3
00:00:49,535 --> 00:00:50,415
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is feeling?

4
00:00:52,236 --> 00:01:00,018
[SPEAKER_01]: And how is that different from, let's say, emotions?

5
00:01:00,038 --> 00:01:06,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's start with sensation, because that's the most obvious difference, I guess.

6
00:01:19,043 --> 00:01:19,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Any ideas?

7
00:01:21,784 --> 00:01:29,608
[SPEAKER_03]: Sensation could be like... any sensation, like sensation of cold, sensation of heat, sensation of... yeah, whatever.

8
00:01:29,668 --> 00:01:39,573
[SPEAKER_03]: And feeling, maybe, like something more... primal, more in the background, like general unpleasantness, or general, like, agreeable, or neither of them.

9
00:01:40,394 --> 00:01:42,035
[SPEAKER_03]: Could be, like, one of the distinctions.

10
00:01:43,736 --> 00:01:46,057
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so, are sensations suffering?

11
00:01:46,517 --> 00:01:48,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Can, can sensations make you suffer?

12
00:01:53,474 --> 00:02:01,922
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, if I cling to them, like, in the sense that I believe that my unpleasantness comes from that, yes, but...

13
00:02:01,962 --> 00:02:08,067
[SPEAKER_00]: So, really, it's not the sensation that makes you suffer, it's the feeling accompanying the sensation that will make you suffer.

14
00:02:08,127 --> 00:02:08,367
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

15
00:02:11,314 --> 00:02:20,242
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that right there is already a primal distinction that would be overlooked in many contemporary practices and views.

16
00:02:20,282 --> 00:02:26,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people would practice in the sense of enduring their sensations.

17
00:02:27,048 --> 00:02:29,250
[SPEAKER_00]: It's quite a common thing.

18
00:02:29,330 --> 00:02:35,836
[SPEAKER_00]: While you're meditating and stuff, like you have a pain in your knee, and you think that your reactions to that

19
00:02:36,937 --> 00:02:41,479
[SPEAKER_00]: You confuse that with the same level as the dukkha that the Buddha was talking about.

20
00:02:41,499 --> 00:02:46,301
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by training yourself to put up with sensations, you think you're training yourself to put up with your feelings.

21
00:02:46,401 --> 00:02:51,963
[SPEAKER_00]: But, I mean, you might, by coincidence, sort of be enduring feelings at the same time.

22
00:02:52,423 --> 00:02:57,565
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that you don't even see the distinction means, like, you're not actually training the same thing.

23
00:02:57,705 --> 00:02:58,005
[SPEAKER_00]: That's right.

24
00:02:58,025 --> 00:03:01,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can't train this understanding by accident.

25
00:03:02,607 --> 00:03:12,971
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're having an unpleasant sensation of your knee hurts or something, well, I mean, technically, you can't even say unpleasant sensation.

26
00:03:13,011 --> 00:03:15,652
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like a moron.

27
00:03:15,732 --> 00:03:28,577
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... it's a... you could say a sensation of heat, a sensation of pain, a sensation of cold, but you can't say unpleasant sensation, because that's already... you're involving the feeling there.

28
00:03:29,198 --> 00:03:29,598
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you...

29
00:03:33,181 --> 00:03:37,145
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unpleasant sensation is already two things.

30
00:03:37,445 --> 00:03:40,788
[SPEAKER_00]: It's perception, which is the sensation, and feeling.

31
00:03:41,629 --> 00:03:48,616
[SPEAKER_01]: And furthermore, even strictly speaking, the notion of sensation is also already somewhat of a confusion.

32
00:03:48,956 --> 00:03:58,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so, you can distinguish in terms of... like, obviously, the way it would be assumed would be more than it really is.

33
00:03:58,518 --> 00:04:00,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Really, a sensation is just a perception of touch.

34
00:04:01,599 --> 00:04:03,559
[SPEAKER_00]: It's one of the five senses, basically.

35
00:04:04,379 --> 00:04:11,281
[SPEAKER_00]: The fifth sense has the counterpart of touches in the same way that the eye has the counterpart of sights.

36
00:04:11,961 --> 00:04:14,022
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what a quote-unquote sensation is.

37
00:04:14,062 --> 00:04:14,742
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a touch.

38
00:04:16,203 --> 00:04:17,265
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, that's what it should be.

39
00:04:17,506 --> 00:04:19,409
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how the suttas describe it, basically.

40
00:04:19,430 --> 00:04:21,473
[SPEAKER_00]: People might kind of put more into it.

41
00:04:21,714 --> 00:04:25,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because then, you see, then there might still be a further confusion about that.

42
00:04:25,722 --> 00:04:27,745
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're used to a sensation in that sense,

43
00:04:28,925 --> 00:04:31,146
[SPEAKER_01]: It can be used in the context of dhamma.

44
00:04:31,726 --> 00:04:56,477
[SPEAKER_01]: But if a scientist is going to measure impulses going up and down your nervous system, or stuff that's happening in your brain, calling that sensation, creates more confusion, because it is conflating what is publicly observable from a scientific point of view, right, impulses traveling up and down your nervous system, with actual things like heat and cold that are experiential,

45
00:04:57,137 --> 00:05:15,767
[SPEAKER_01]: And fully outside of the scope of the sciences, so the notion of sensation itself, as has already been established by the existential phenomenologists, is sort of a hybrid notion that tends to blur what is first-person experiential with what is third-person.

46
00:05:18,447 --> 00:05:19,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-experiential, let's put it like that.

47
00:05:20,549 --> 00:05:22,110
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's one confusion.

48
00:05:22,410 --> 00:05:30,257
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we should clear that up by saying that sensation, the way we're going to discuss it, is on the level of perception, which is fully within dhamma.

49
00:05:30,277 --> 00:05:36,042
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we don't need to discuss what's happening inside the brain or the nervous system, because that's totally irrelevant for practicing Buddhist teaching.

50
00:05:36,702 --> 00:05:42,267
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that notion of sensation specified should be further distinguished from feeling.

51
00:05:43,408 --> 00:05:43,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Right?

52
00:05:43,888 --> 00:05:44,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

53
00:05:45,269 --> 00:06:09,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that sensation... yeah, like you said, because of putting science where it doesn't really belong, really, not that it's wrong in itself, but putting it where it doesn't belong, then you would make more out of this thing, which is absolutely not special in any sense, like sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, thoughts.

54
00:06:10,096 --> 00:06:11,797
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, in regard to this, you have feeling.

55
00:06:12,437 --> 00:06:16,619
[SPEAKER_00]: None of those six things are particularly, kind of, more special than the other.

56
00:06:16,719 --> 00:06:17,100
[SPEAKER_00]: That's right.

57
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:18,500
[SPEAKER_00]: All of them are just perception.

58
00:06:19,141 --> 00:06:36,050
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but yeah, if you start assuming that, kind of, the sensations go into your brain and stuff, then... And then, furthermore, you're assuming that freedom from suffering would be something that you can measure based on the... whatever chemicals or reactions in the brain.

59
00:06:36,130 --> 00:06:39,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, all of what you're measuring is basically, eh...

60
00:06:39,932 --> 00:06:54,506
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, feeling is always something that's personal.

61
00:06:54,826 --> 00:06:59,050
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always something that is basically from that first-person point of view.

62
00:06:59,090 --> 00:07:03,194
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the part that a scientist might be able to observe would be

63
00:07:05,535 --> 00:07:05,855
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe.

64
00:07:06,375 --> 00:07:17,257
[SPEAKER_00]: The heat, cold... Like, if there are some kind of nervous impulses that represent those sensations, those touches, then, yeah, that could be measured.

65
00:07:17,277 --> 00:07:26,159
[SPEAKER_00]: But your personal relationship with that, that could... the level where really the development of mind happens, that you wouldn't be able to measure that on account of that.

66
00:07:26,379 --> 00:07:31,220
[SPEAKER_01]: And even then, you could say a scientist might measure that something is 20 degrees Celsius,

67
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:34,664
[SPEAKER_01]: But whether that is for you, warm or cold, is still another matter.

68
00:07:36,827 --> 00:07:37,568
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's...

69
00:07:38,389 --> 00:07:47,721
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the suttas talk about how, for example, like the mind of an arahant, he would say, like, nobody would be able to tell it apart.

70
00:07:49,363 --> 00:07:53,386
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody would be able to to determine where he stands, basically.

71
00:07:53,946 --> 00:08:06,457
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I mean, with a sensation, kind of, if it were on that level, then you just measure this thing and you can tell who's an arahant based on this very trivial, kind of, mundane thing.

72
00:08:06,517 --> 00:08:11,020
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and furthermore, like, if your body just... What happens if your body

73
00:08:12,161 --> 00:08:22,609
[SPEAKER_00]: If those chemicals and stuff stop working the way they did, which lead you to what you think is freedom from suffering, then arhantship would be kind of reversible.

74
00:08:22,689 --> 00:08:24,110
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be impermanent.

75
00:08:24,330 --> 00:08:24,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Dependent.

76
00:08:24,891 --> 00:08:25,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Dependent, yeah.

77
00:08:25,551 --> 00:08:26,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Dependent on form.

78
00:08:27,173 --> 00:08:28,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally dependent on form.

79
00:08:28,554 --> 00:08:28,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

80
00:08:29,154 --> 00:08:32,036
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can that be what the Buddha was actually teaching?

81
00:08:32,076 --> 00:08:32,677
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible.

82
00:08:33,397 --> 00:08:42,586
[SPEAKER_00]: So, feelings, how to know what you're feeling and how to tell it apart from perception?

83
00:08:42,646 --> 00:08:53,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, just imagine any time in your life where, if you, let's say, you had a reward coming on account of putting up with an unpleasant physical sensation,

84
00:08:54,978 --> 00:08:57,300
[SPEAKER_00]: And, like, you were really close to getting that reward.

85
00:08:58,821 --> 00:09:17,536
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know, I mean, even doing sports and stuff, like, it's physically unpleasant, but the mental pleasure is there on the level of feeling, because you expect to win the competition, you know, all the praise and honor that's going to come out of you winning this.

86
00:09:17,596 --> 00:09:19,298
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is a pleasant feeling there.

87
00:09:19,958 --> 00:09:24,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Even though the sensation might be very unpleasant on a physical level.

88
00:09:24,959 --> 00:09:33,542
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that is the prime example of that distinction between feelings and sensations.

89
00:09:33,582 --> 00:09:40,344
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, all the delight and lust that the Buddha talked about is towards your feelings.

90
00:09:40,904 --> 00:09:56,269
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if a sensation is not... well, the light and lust and aversion for that matter... if a sensation is not producing a feeling, let's say a pleasant sensation, is not producing a feeling, a corresponding feeling,

91
00:09:56,989 --> 00:10:00,491
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to it, then, there can be no delight in that sensation.

92
00:10:00,511 --> 00:10:06,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can be eating, you know, the best food in the world, for example, but you're in a really bad mood.

93
00:10:06,814 --> 00:10:09,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, something bad really happened, just happened to you.

94
00:10:10,136 --> 00:10:17,659
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't be able to delight in it, because the feeling that is reigning over your experience is unpleasant.

95
00:10:18,180 --> 00:10:19,840
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's not compatible.

96
00:10:20,401 --> 00:10:22,662
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's a really unpleasant situation,

97
00:10:23,422 --> 00:10:28,589
[SPEAKER_00]: Even though the sensation, quote-unquote, the the taste is pleasant, you won't be able to delight in it.

98
00:10:29,470 --> 00:10:37,901
[SPEAKER_01]: So... Yeah, so we could say that there's feeling present for, let's say, each and every sense base, but there's always one

99
00:10:41,704 --> 00:11:01,301
[SPEAKER_01]: So, practically speaking, it's probably most useful to just ask the question, what is the overall feeling that is enduring right now, from my experience as a whole, and not start getting lost in particular sense spaces, but just recognize

100
00:11:02,111 --> 00:11:06,734
[SPEAKER_01]: That there will always be an overall feeling that you find yourself within, so to speak.

101
00:11:06,974 --> 00:11:07,214
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

102
00:11:07,374 --> 00:11:15,219
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha said, on an occasion where you're feeling pleasure, you cannot be feeling pain at the same time, on the level of feeling.

103
00:11:15,759 --> 00:11:20,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensation, like particular stuff, can be coming and going.

104
00:11:21,262 --> 00:11:24,724
[SPEAKER_00]: But on that general level, you always only have one.

105
00:11:24,924 --> 00:11:27,566
[SPEAKER_00]: He said that in Majjhima, 74, I think.

106
00:11:27,726 --> 00:11:31,148
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, on an occasion where somebody feels a pleasant feeling,

107
00:11:31,668 --> 00:11:37,893
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't feel an unpleasant or neither pleasant or unpleasant feeling because it is exclusive.

108
00:11:38,193 --> 00:11:40,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it is...

109
00:11:40,554 --> 00:11:46,999
[SPEAKER_00]: In a practical sense, you can realize you can't be doing well and doing bad at the same time.

110
00:11:47,639 --> 00:11:48,680
[SPEAKER_00]: It has to be one of the two.

111
00:11:49,201 --> 00:11:54,084
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that kind of summarizes what a feeling is.

112
00:11:54,124 --> 00:11:58,787
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of... What is the answer to the question, how am I doing right now?

113
00:12:03,036 --> 00:12:04,098
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not like the...

114
00:12:04,118 --> 00:12:12,930
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, you can kind of look at that and read it and say, well, I don't know, for instance, right now, like, I don't know,

115
00:12:13,437 --> 00:12:17,198
[SPEAKER_04]: My body is warm, which is pleasant, but my knee hurts, which is unpleasant.

116
00:12:17,498 --> 00:12:18,858
[SPEAKER_04]: Therefore, that's not true.

117
00:12:19,098 --> 00:12:21,139
[SPEAKER_04]: But, like, it's on that more general mood level.

118
00:12:21,159 --> 00:12:21,479
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

119
00:12:21,519 --> 00:12:24,720
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, how am I, in the most general sense, how am I doing?

120
00:12:24,880 --> 00:12:33,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, like, would I like this to continue or would I like it to change?

121
00:12:34,142 --> 00:12:39,603
[SPEAKER_00]: And if the answer is I would like it to change, then you can know there is some degree of unpleasantness there.

122
00:12:39,663 --> 00:12:39,903
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

123
00:12:40,543 --> 00:12:43,644
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasure is what you want more of.

124
00:12:44,504 --> 00:12:46,184
[SPEAKER_00]: This pleasure is what you want less of.

125
00:12:46,945 --> 00:13:09,230
[SPEAKER_00]: And then there's the third one, which is neither pleasant nor unpleasant feeling, which is essentially the one that you wouldn't even be able to recognize unless you have been dwelling in seclusion, because the neither pleasant nor unpleasant feeling by someone who's not restrained is essentially automatically covered up with either pleasure or pain, because

126
00:13:10,553 --> 00:13:11,647
[SPEAKER_00]: It's so kind of...

127
00:13:13,557 --> 00:13:14,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Slippery.

128
00:13:15,317 --> 00:13:21,019
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like... it's below the threshold of the things that you're used to looking at.

129
00:13:21,039 --> 00:13:26,880
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, when it's there, it automatically... you just can't stand it and you turn it into something else.

130
00:13:27,240 --> 00:13:31,061
[SPEAKER_00]: You get busy, you get distracted, you get... you start thinking about stuff.

131
00:13:31,681 --> 00:13:33,261
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just stay with it.

132
00:13:33,281 --> 00:13:38,802
[SPEAKER_01]: So... Yeah, I think it says when it's not known, it's experienced as unpleasant.

133
00:13:38,842 --> 00:13:39,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

134
00:13:39,342 --> 00:13:42,783
[SPEAKER_01]: But when that feeling is known, it's actually experienced as pleasant.

135
00:13:42,883 --> 00:13:43,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

136
00:13:43,943 --> 00:14:00,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's the lack of disturbance that, for an ordinary person that's used to pleasure of the wrong kind, that lack of disturbance, really, is experienced more as a kind of, like, boredom.

137
00:14:00,967 --> 00:14:03,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, kind of... Anxiety.

138
00:14:03,848 --> 00:14:04,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

139
00:14:04,268 --> 00:14:05,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Anxiety, uneasiness.

140
00:14:06,188 --> 00:14:06,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Sinking.

141
00:14:06,588 --> 00:14:07,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Sinking, yeah.

142
00:14:07,849 --> 00:14:08,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is unpleasant, yeah.

143
00:14:09,029 --> 00:14:12,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you... it's like... like you've been running

144
00:14:13,750 --> 00:14:17,133
[SPEAKER_00]: With a certain momentum, and then, suddenly, like, it stops.

145
00:14:17,454 --> 00:14:19,556
[SPEAKER_00]: But you still need to, kind of, keep going.

146
00:14:19,576 --> 00:14:27,763
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you overlook the fact that, wait, when I actually stop running, as I've been doing before, it is actually more peaceful.

147
00:14:28,264 --> 00:14:28,704
[SPEAKER_00]: But because

148
00:14:29,605 --> 00:14:33,568
[SPEAKER_00]: The momentum, kind of, is the thing that you put first.

149
00:14:34,248 --> 00:14:36,770
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't see that, actually, this is better.

150
00:14:36,810 --> 00:14:44,195
[SPEAKER_00]: So, neither pleasant nor unpleasant feeling is what a person then needs to start to discern.

151
00:14:44,215 --> 00:14:56,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, pleasure and pain is essentially... anybody can tell, if they... with just some basic instruction, when they're feeling pleasant, where they're feeling unpleasant, but that neutral one is...

152
00:14:57,944 --> 00:14:58,184
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

153
00:15:00,460 --> 00:15:10,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, eh... since we cleared up what is the difference between feeling and the sensations, then what would be emotions?

154
00:15:13,188 --> 00:15:17,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, perhaps you can ask, like, are you responsible for your emotions?

155
00:15:18,552 --> 00:15:20,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are they things that happen to you?

156
00:15:21,134 --> 00:15:22,114
[SPEAKER_01]: What do people here say?

157
00:15:27,118 --> 00:15:29,199
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I guess if I was responsible, I would be

158
00:15:32,966 --> 00:15:39,511
[SPEAKER_03]: If I was controlling all processes of it, I would never experience anger or things like that, I guess.

159
00:15:40,232 --> 00:15:44,895
[SPEAKER_03]: So, as I said, I'm at least not fully responsible for them to appear.

160
00:15:47,617 --> 00:15:48,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Anyone else?

161
00:15:48,518 --> 00:15:49,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Any ideas?

162
00:15:50,379 --> 00:15:55,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's a common view that emotions are something that happen, you know,

163
00:15:55,840 --> 00:15:57,921
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm struck by jealousy or something like this.

164
00:15:58,741 --> 00:15:59,502
[SPEAKER_02]: Common expression.

165
00:16:01,303 --> 00:16:03,504
[SPEAKER_01]: But I think that this is worthwhile discussing.

166
00:16:04,544 --> 00:16:04,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Right?

167
00:16:05,225 --> 00:16:07,906
[SPEAKER_01]: So, emotion, literally the word, right?

168
00:16:08,186 --> 00:16:13,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Emotion means the mind being moved by things or disturbed.

169
00:16:13,909 --> 00:16:16,090
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what emotion means.

170
00:16:17,590 --> 00:16:24,754
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, it seems to me that it is crucial to recognize that all emotions are intentional.

171
00:16:26,100 --> 00:16:35,326
[SPEAKER_01]: In the sense that they're always about something, but that intentionality should be strictly separated from the cause of your emotions.

172
00:16:37,027 --> 00:16:45,152
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for example, someone breaks my favorite coffee cup and I'm angry with that person for having broken my coffee cup.

173
00:16:45,272 --> 00:16:48,234
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the emotion of anger is about.

174
00:16:48,934 --> 00:16:50,135
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the intentionality.

175
00:16:51,036 --> 00:16:54,838
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't mean that my coffee cup

176
00:16:55,727 --> 00:17:01,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Having been broken is the cause of my being angry.

177
00:17:01,572 --> 00:17:03,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are two totally separate things.

178
00:17:05,115 --> 00:17:10,879
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is then the cause of me getting angry when someone breaks my favorite coffee cup?

179
00:17:12,080 --> 00:17:12,801
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would that happen?

180
00:17:19,372 --> 00:17:22,034
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, that's exactly what I was going to say.

181
00:17:22,114 --> 00:17:23,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, very good.

182
00:17:23,275 --> 00:17:30,861
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, so, on the level of emotions then, like, what needs to be there in order for that emotion to flood up to begin with?

183
00:17:32,477 --> 00:17:34,098
[SPEAKER_01]: So, okay, maybe one step back.

184
00:17:34,178 --> 00:17:36,860
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is an emotion, like anger, which is unpleasant.

185
00:17:37,280 --> 00:17:39,841
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that is how we can already separate emotion from feeling.

186
00:17:40,322 --> 00:17:46,185
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all emotions are already, let's say, complete phenomenon.

187
00:17:46,665 --> 00:17:47,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's actually what phenomenon means.

188
00:17:48,026 --> 00:17:50,687
[SPEAKER_01]: It means, like, the totality of something which is present.

189
00:17:51,288 --> 00:17:54,490
[SPEAKER_01]: And one aspect of it is that it is either pleasant, unpleasant, or neither.

190
00:17:55,070 --> 00:17:58,072
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how we can separate feeling, again, from emotion.

191
00:17:58,853 --> 00:18:06,958
[SPEAKER_01]: But now we can ask also the question, why is... based on what is one liable to emotions, which are always unpleasant, by the way?

192
00:18:08,179 --> 00:18:14,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that is also what we can discuss, but I just said it, like, emotions are always unpleasant, but why do they happen?

193
00:18:18,125 --> 00:18:18,586
[SPEAKER_03]: Attachment.

194
00:18:20,207 --> 00:18:21,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

195
00:18:22,128 --> 00:18:28,012
[SPEAKER_01]: If I'm attached to my favorite coffee cup, then somebody breaking it will make me angry,

196
00:18:29,167 --> 00:18:31,530
[SPEAKER_01]: With that person for having broken my coffee cup.

197
00:18:31,570 --> 00:18:46,370
[SPEAKER_01]: But if I confuse the intentionality that my anger is about my coffee cup having been broken with the cause, namely my attachment, if that's... if that's conflated, I will never be able to free myself from emotions, because stuff in the world just happens.

198
00:18:47,111 --> 00:18:48,132
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot prevent that.

199
00:18:48,552 --> 00:19:02,063
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I do not want to be liable to suffer the unpleasantness of emotions, rather than assuming full control over the world, which is impossible, you should actually move in the opposite direction and start questioning whether you're still attached to things.

200
00:19:02,869 --> 00:19:09,191
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the... because then, if you say it's because you're attached to the coffee cup, then it might seem like...

201
00:19:09,211 --> 00:19:12,752
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I need to find everything that... every single thing that I'm attached to and then give it up.

202
00:19:12,792 --> 00:19:17,213
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how could you... why are you more acutely attached to?

203
00:19:19,514 --> 00:19:21,254
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, the assumption of feeling.

204
00:19:21,374 --> 00:19:21,654
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

205
00:19:21,814 --> 00:19:23,375
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the most general.

206
00:19:23,695 --> 00:19:24,335
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course.

207
00:19:24,355 --> 00:19:27,836
[SPEAKER_00]: So, really, you're attached to not experiencing this pleasure.

208
00:19:28,596 --> 00:19:30,637
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might say the coffee cup

209
00:19:32,384 --> 00:19:45,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Because many times when people say, like, um, uh, I shouldn't be attached to this, what they think of it as is more like, I shouldn't, for example, feel this pleasure when the coffee cup is broken.

210
00:19:46,058 --> 00:19:46,859
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not on you.

211
00:19:46,899 --> 00:19:51,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, um, you feel this pleasure because that is part of the arrogance.

212
00:19:52,645 --> 00:19:52,925
[SPEAKER_00]: It is

213
00:19:54,127 --> 00:20:00,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's not the coffee cup, even if you give everything up, you live under a bridge, and you have no possessions, quote-unquote.

214
00:20:00,853 --> 00:20:06,339
[SPEAKER_00]: When this body breaks, you're gonna feel displeasure, at least on that account.

215
00:20:06,379 --> 00:20:09,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the way to free yourself fully is not to have attachment.

216
00:20:10,203 --> 00:20:17,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Towards what you feel and on that more kind of elemental level.

217
00:20:17,426 --> 00:20:23,029
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would fix every problem by...

218
00:20:25,790 --> 00:20:30,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Giving up, as you said, the resistance to unpleasant feeling.

219
00:20:30,693 --> 00:20:34,976
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the fundamental... And that is the cause of the emotion.

220
00:20:35,436 --> 00:20:40,219
[SPEAKER_00]: If there isn't any resistance to the unpleasant feeling,

221
00:20:40,899 --> 00:20:45,943
[SPEAKER_00]: There cannot be anger, there cannot be jealousy, there cannot be... but the unpleasant feeling is still there.

222
00:20:46,043 --> 00:20:46,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

223
00:20:46,684 --> 00:20:51,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, because still, I don't know, you saw something factually as...

224
00:20:51,988 --> 00:20:56,532
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the Buddha gave the simile in, I think, Majjhima Nikaya 140.

225
00:20:56,592 --> 00:21:04,898
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, it's like if two logs or pieces of wood were to be rubbed together and then they produce heat.

226
00:21:04,938 --> 00:21:08,281
[SPEAKER_00]: For as long as you're rubbing them together, they're going to be producing heat.

227
00:21:08,401 --> 00:21:09,082
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same way,

228
00:21:09,762 --> 00:21:18,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever there is a pressure or a contact that is to be experienced as pleasant, pleasant feeling will remain.

229
00:21:18,445 --> 00:21:18,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

230
00:21:18,945 --> 00:21:26,848
[SPEAKER_00]: For as long as... and so, you have no control over what situation is leading to an unpleasant feeling.

231
00:21:27,028 --> 00:21:30,829
[SPEAKER_00]: That will depend on various factors that are not in your control.

232
00:21:30,869 --> 00:21:36,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you might have been born with preference for a certain food, for example, that another person hates.

233
00:21:37,791 --> 00:21:45,117
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, for as long as you're in contact with this, you will be experiencing pleasure, whereas the other person will not.

234
00:21:45,357 --> 00:21:56,866
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, your say or your job is, regardless of what feeling comes, you must be able not to lean towards it or resist it.

235
00:21:57,747 --> 00:22:02,191
[SPEAKER_00]: And on that level, then, it doesn't matter, like, what specific thing it is.

236
00:22:03,152 --> 00:22:03,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

237
00:22:03,732 --> 00:22:07,355
[SPEAKER_01]: That is, generally, for basically all phenomena,

238
00:22:09,692 --> 00:22:14,817
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is the level where basically the practice of dhamma applies.

239
00:22:15,437 --> 00:22:17,860
[SPEAKER_01]: And one, let's say,

240
00:22:19,341 --> 00:22:26,486
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, where it also applies is with emotions, which is just one kind of phenomenon that also happens in people's lives.

241
00:22:26,506 --> 00:22:34,192
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anger, as you said, envy, jealousy, loneliness, self-pity, and so on.

242
00:22:34,312 --> 00:22:35,753
[SPEAKER_01]: They're all unpleasant.

243
00:22:36,474 --> 00:22:43,739
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, once we have cleared up the distinction between the intentional structure and the cause of the emotion, by being the

244
00:22:44,313 --> 00:22:53,176
[SPEAKER_01]: The resistance towards not wanting to experience unpleasant feelings, one basically should go about it, as we have discussed in other talks already.

245
00:22:54,057 --> 00:23:00,479
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you see, but the difficulty is then that there can be a natural tendency to wanting to get rid of the emotions because they are unpleasant.

246
00:23:00,879 --> 00:23:03,921
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I shouldn't feel angry.

247
00:23:04,241 --> 00:23:07,202
[SPEAKER_01]: It's so childish to feel jealous.

248
00:23:07,922 --> 00:23:08,602
[SPEAKER_01]: And all those things.

249
00:23:09,062 --> 00:23:09,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereas, actually,

250
00:23:11,013 --> 00:23:20,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that phenomenon called emotion is still what happens, but one wants to then realize that it does still happen on account of not having understood that feeling.

251
00:23:20,965 --> 00:23:21,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

252
00:23:21,286 --> 00:23:26,172
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I would say then that one is 100% fully responsible

253
00:23:26,712 --> 00:23:28,833
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's a there's a sort of a double

254
00:23:52,003 --> 00:23:59,008
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on the one hand, you are fully responsible, but at the same time, you don't have control over them, as you said before.

255
00:23:59,048 --> 00:24:01,629
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like you decide to be angry.

256
00:24:01,889 --> 00:24:03,010
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

257
00:24:03,350 --> 00:24:04,952
[SPEAKER_00]: For a puṭrjuna, that's how it works.

258
00:24:05,172 --> 00:24:09,454
[SPEAKER_00]: For a person who doesn't see the sign of their mind, means a person who doesn't see

259
00:24:10,375 --> 00:24:21,080
[SPEAKER_00]: How they resist, or how they delight in their feelings, means they don't see, as the Buddha would call it, the fuel that's powering these emotions.

260
00:24:21,200 --> 00:24:32,285
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, what they would be trying to do, as you said just now, is, like, try to get rid of the emotions directly, by countering them with this, like, when I have anger, I counter it with the emotion of metta.

261
00:24:32,525 --> 00:24:32,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

262
00:24:33,025 --> 00:24:47,655
[SPEAKER_00]: But, it's really... the only way to actually solve the issues that have managed it is to see and recognize that intention to either... how the mind is either leaning towards or leaning away from the feeling.

263
00:24:48,175 --> 00:24:57,261
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, don't worry about the emotion because you can't... that is arisen on account of what you did in the past and you can't, like, undo that.

264
00:24:58,281 --> 00:25:03,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Really, emotions, you could say, when the suttas talk about the results of your actions.

265
00:25:04,802 --> 00:25:13,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, obviously, there is a component of like, you know, you are stingy and then, as the Buddha would say, in the next life you would be reborn as a poor person or whatever.

266
00:25:13,764 --> 00:25:19,705
[SPEAKER_00]: But, more importantly, you could say emotions are the results of your actions.

267
00:25:20,206 --> 00:25:22,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Your action in the sense of resisting feeling.

268
00:25:22,486 --> 00:25:26,607
[SPEAKER_00]: So, resisting feeling, for example, having resisted feeling in the past,

269
00:25:27,528 --> 00:25:31,290
[SPEAKER_00]: The result of that is, when you encounter this pleasure, anger will be there.

270
00:25:31,810 --> 00:25:32,431
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a result.

271
00:25:32,451 --> 00:25:33,531
[SPEAKER_02]: Manifesting in this way.

272
00:25:33,732 --> 00:25:33,952
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

273
00:25:34,052 --> 00:25:35,012
[SPEAKER_02]: These particular ways.

274
00:25:35,272 --> 00:25:35,873
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

275
00:25:36,033 --> 00:25:36,673
[SPEAKER_00]: That's that.

276
00:25:36,733 --> 00:25:41,296
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's like a foundation of resistance that then gets manifested in these.

277
00:25:41,716 --> 00:25:47,760
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if it happens to be that something bad or something annoying happens, you're going to get angry.

278
00:25:48,600 --> 00:25:55,925
[SPEAKER_00]: If you see, I don't know, pleasure, what's going to happen is you're going to be craving for it.

279
00:25:56,466 --> 00:26:00,749
[SPEAKER_00]: If you see... if you're sleepy, that's going to result in you getting lazy.

280
00:26:01,469 --> 00:26:07,574
[SPEAKER_00]: If your mind gets, you know, has a lot of movement in it, that's going to result in restlessness.

281
00:26:08,174 --> 00:26:11,216
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you see, what I'm describing now is basically emotions.

282
00:26:11,436 --> 00:26:12,037
[SPEAKER_00]: You could take it

283
00:26:12,537 --> 00:26:14,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Kind of on the same level as the five hindrances.

284
00:26:14,558 --> 00:26:15,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yes, yes.

285
00:26:15,558 --> 00:26:22,720
[SPEAKER_00]: It comes up as a result of things that you've been doing in the past, but when it's there, you can't just cover it up and get rid of it.

286
00:26:22,780 --> 00:26:24,040
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to remove the cause.

287
00:26:24,960 --> 00:26:28,601
[SPEAKER_00]: And the cause is... the cause is right now.

288
00:26:28,661 --> 00:26:33,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the cause is that even now, look, I'm trying to get rid of this emotion.

289
00:26:33,723 --> 00:26:33,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

290
00:26:34,003 --> 00:26:35,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I'm still resisting.

291
00:26:35,463 --> 00:26:39,524
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm still maintaining the same principle of resisting unpleasant feeling.

292
00:26:40,065 --> 00:26:40,845
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, like,

293
00:26:41,785 --> 00:26:47,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything that you might do, you replace this with that, and you antidote with loving-kindness and stuff.

294
00:26:47,893 --> 00:26:51,076
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is still within the fact that you resist this pleasure.

295
00:26:51,257 --> 00:26:52,498
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're feeding the same thing.

296
00:26:53,019 --> 00:27:01,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you successfully manage to, you know, get the emotion that you want, the fact is you're still

297
00:27:01,969 --> 00:27:05,031
[SPEAKER_00]: Not actually addressing the the fundamental cause.

298
00:27:05,071 --> 00:27:10,735
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, in the future, when other unpleasant feelings manifest, you're going to have to go through the same thing again.

299
00:27:11,176 --> 00:27:14,198
[SPEAKER_00]: Emotion is going to be there and you're going to have to counter it with something else.

300
00:27:14,318 --> 00:27:23,744
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you actually address the the craving for or against feeling, then emotions would not arise in the first place.

301
00:27:24,485 --> 00:27:27,027
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't need to counter or manage or do anything.

302
00:27:27,547 --> 00:27:30,769
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's kind of the... Yeah, but you see...

303
00:27:32,151 --> 00:27:32,832
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all correct.

304
00:27:33,213 --> 00:27:44,473
[SPEAKER_01]: But would you not say, like, for example, if somebody still has the view that anger might serve a useful purpose, that there are occasions where it is in your welfare to be angry,

305
00:27:45,367 --> 00:27:50,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, will somebody be able to contemplate the dhamma correctly on the level of feeling, if they still hold a view like that?

306
00:27:51,551 --> 00:27:55,173
[SPEAKER_01]: So, perhaps we should make a slight...

307
00:27:55,313 --> 00:28:07,481
[SPEAKER_01]: This is all correct, but I think to go one step slightly back, then there is a whole area there, where there is a ton of confusion, where people might think, no, no, no, if this or that happens in my life, certainly I'm justified in being angry.

308
00:28:07,541 --> 00:28:12,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, those views need to go, because to the extent that you allow room

309
00:28:14,712 --> 00:28:23,541
[SPEAKER_01]: For anger being useful, you will not be able to rightly endure the displeasure of the anger when it arises.

310
00:28:23,601 --> 00:28:25,663
[SPEAKER_01]: That anger won't go.

311
00:28:26,424 --> 00:28:35,954
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one also has views about emotions and it seems to me that it is quite useful to regard them as generally quite useless and

312
00:28:36,875 --> 00:28:55,908
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but I think that might also be confused, because, again, if you don't see that distinction between this pleasure, between emotion and feeling, it might sound like, oh, yeah, I shouldn't have emotions anymore.

313
00:28:56,148 --> 00:29:00,591
[SPEAKER_00]: It sounds like you should become like some sort of grey blob of nothing, basically.

314
00:29:00,631 --> 00:29:01,732
[SPEAKER_00]: But no, it's...

315
00:29:03,253 --> 00:29:04,754
[SPEAKER_00]: Emotions are

316
00:29:05,815 --> 00:29:09,158
[SPEAKER_00]: On top of, like, gratuitously added on top of the feelings.

317
00:29:09,879 --> 00:29:16,424
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and feelings in and of themselves, when you feel something unpleasant, you will

318
00:29:18,017 --> 00:29:25,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Without emotion, be able to do something to fix that situation, if it is something that's not unwholesome, for example.

319
00:29:25,324 --> 00:29:30,549
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as the Buddha would say, when his back would hurt, he would lie down.

320
00:29:30,809 --> 00:29:41,979
[SPEAKER_00]: When his... when somebody would say something really foolish, like misrepresenting him, he would have the capacity to tell them off and strongly rebuke them.

321
00:29:42,660 --> 00:29:43,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Without having emotions.

322
00:29:44,466 --> 00:29:48,197
[SPEAKER_00]: So, emotion is... the problem with emotion is that it's something that...

323
00:29:49,412 --> 00:29:50,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Kind of like you're subjected to.

324
00:29:51,434 --> 00:29:53,375
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the main characteristic of emotions.

325
00:29:53,395 --> 00:29:55,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you're not really in control of this.

326
00:29:55,777 --> 00:30:01,641
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might be able to say, like, oh, I need this so that I can do such and such.

327
00:30:01,741 --> 00:30:04,143
[SPEAKER_00]: But really, you're not using it.

328
00:30:04,283 --> 00:30:05,064
[SPEAKER_00]: It's using you.

329
00:30:05,424 --> 00:30:06,165
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole thing.

330
00:30:06,185 --> 00:30:12,710
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't have, like, you might believe that you're using your anger for a beneficial.

331
00:30:12,770 --> 00:30:16,273
[SPEAKER_00]: But really, if you ask yourself, I don't have a say in this,

332
00:30:16,933 --> 00:30:21,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if I tell myself to stop being angry, you won't be able to do it by choice.

333
00:30:21,455 --> 00:30:23,816
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's really controlling you.

334
00:30:23,977 --> 00:30:34,502
[SPEAKER_00]: So, well, to some extent, I guess, it would depend on some degree of faith, but at the same time, like, clarifying the actual meaning of these terms.

335
00:30:34,562 --> 00:30:42,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, see, for example, people who have the view that if you can't see the distinction between these three things,

336
00:30:43,346 --> 00:30:45,308
[SPEAKER_00]: And you think that feelings are sensations.

337
00:30:46,008 --> 00:30:49,110
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the whole thing just gets messed up.

338
00:30:49,130 --> 00:30:56,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Because how can they... how can then you understand the statement that you get rid of the emotions, but you still have feelings?

339
00:30:58,057 --> 00:31:00,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, that would sound like, oh, I just have sensations.

340
00:31:00,279 --> 00:31:03,881
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, mentally, there is no pleasure and pain.

341
00:31:03,901 --> 00:31:09,586
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what people would think of as an arahants, as a kind of robots, that that only has sensations.

342
00:31:09,826 --> 00:31:11,127
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is no feeling.

343
00:31:11,747 --> 00:31:20,778
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole thing would get scrambled up, but when you see the difference between these three things, you see there can still be pleasure.

344
00:31:22,416 --> 00:31:31,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a mental experience of pleasure, there can still be a mental experience of displeasure, and those things are always, as you said, intentional.

345
00:31:32,062 --> 00:31:39,026
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there will always be, eh... to put it coarsely, like an object that this thing is directed towards.

346
00:31:39,046 --> 00:31:42,488
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you will be able to make choices in regard to that object.

347
00:31:43,128 --> 00:31:45,190
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what then is the need for emotion?

348
00:31:45,210 --> 00:31:48,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if something needs to be done, you have all you need.

349
00:31:48,853 --> 00:31:51,055
[SPEAKER_00]: You have the feeling and you have the intention.

350
00:31:51,115 --> 00:32:00,284
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the emotion is kind of, really, might seem like, like, kind of, you might justify it after the fact.

351
00:32:01,105 --> 00:32:07,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, because you can't get rid of it, then you convince yourself that you need to have it or something.

352
00:32:07,270 --> 00:32:09,072
[SPEAKER_00]: But really, if you're honest with yourself,

353
00:32:10,854 --> 00:32:12,992
[SPEAKER_00]: If I had a choice not to have this...

354
00:32:15,542 --> 00:32:16,562
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole point.

355
00:32:16,662 --> 00:32:22,204
[SPEAKER_01]: You would be much better off without emotions, but that doesn't mean that there can't be any feeling, because there will be.

356
00:32:22,825 --> 00:32:29,787
[SPEAKER_01]: Nor does it mean that there can't be any sensations either, because there will still be heat and cold and all those things.

357
00:32:29,827 --> 00:32:31,347
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, that's not the issue either.

358
00:32:31,568 --> 00:32:33,908
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind you, some people might say, like...

359
00:32:33,928 --> 00:32:41,331
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm sure some people, if you ask them, you give them a button or something that says, press this and you won't have emotions anymore.

360
00:32:41,371 --> 00:32:41,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Well,

361
00:32:42,371 --> 00:32:50,936
[SPEAKER_00]: A good amount of people might be scared of that because of their own assumption of what their own existence is, basically.

362
00:32:51,016 --> 00:33:01,101
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, they would think not having emotions is just going to be this kind of torture, bleak, kind of lifeless states.

363
00:33:01,481 --> 00:33:04,863
[SPEAKER_00]: But really, it is where the... like, everything that you're seeking

364
00:33:05,723 --> 00:33:14,411
[SPEAKER_00]: in your life, by... completely the wrong way, really, is because you are essentially trying to get rid of your emotions.

365
00:33:14,591 --> 00:33:18,055
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you are already moved, in the first place, by things in the world.

366
00:33:18,415 --> 00:33:25,041
[SPEAKER_01]: And the question is, is it pleasurable to live with the mind that is moved by random things happening in the world?

367
00:33:25,201 --> 00:33:25,361
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

368
00:33:25,902 --> 00:33:29,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, obviously, an intelligent person would come to the conclusion that it isn't.

369
00:33:29,765 --> 00:33:29,926
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

370
00:33:30,506 --> 00:33:30,786
[SPEAKER_02]: But that...

371
00:33:30,806 --> 00:33:31,307
[SPEAKER_02]: I could say that.

372
00:33:34,449 --> 00:33:48,723
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you... if people basically see that emotions is a kind of movement and depending on it, so that becomes very core of existence, being, yeah?

373
00:33:49,404 --> 00:33:53,588
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why it's so difficult to imagine not having emotions.

374
00:33:54,248 --> 00:34:01,832
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you go to the extreme of basically imagining that this is, yeah, lifeless or... You go to the other extreme.

375
00:34:02,032 --> 00:34:06,414
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha said there are only two extremes that people with the rear view can go.

376
00:34:06,674 --> 00:34:14,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Either existence, so like, I delight in everything, I welcome my emotions, I act out of them, and then non-existence.

377
00:34:14,337 --> 00:34:16,758
[SPEAKER_00]: No emotions, no nothing, just black.

378
00:34:17,119 --> 00:34:17,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

379
00:34:17,519 --> 00:34:19,320
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't see the middle way.

380
00:34:20,540 --> 00:34:23,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Get rid of the emotions, but all the feelings are perfectly fine.

381
00:34:24,183 --> 00:34:26,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is not even your fault.

382
00:34:26,884 --> 00:34:27,905
[SPEAKER_00]: Then feelings come up.

383
00:34:28,245 --> 00:34:29,386
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just a constituent.

384
00:34:29,406 --> 00:34:30,526
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it is.

385
00:34:31,927 --> 00:34:36,650
[SPEAKER_00]: So, really, with this emotion thing, it's like what you would call the Stockholm Syndrome.

386
00:34:37,351 --> 00:34:46,096
[SPEAKER_00]: Like people might kind of, you know, be saying that they're happy with this, while they're being held hostage by their own emotions.

387
00:34:47,797 --> 00:34:54,245
[SPEAKER_04]: That kind of reaction to the idea of not having emotions, is an emotional reaction.

388
00:34:54,305 --> 00:34:54,505
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

389
00:34:54,525 --> 00:34:59,851
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your captor, kind of convincing you, oh, no, it's bad outside, don't go out there.

390
00:35:00,011 --> 00:35:00,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

391
00:35:01,613 --> 00:35:07,039
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's really like, it's something that needs to be contemplated to see

392
00:35:09,101 --> 00:35:14,245
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said, it takes discernment to value nibbāna.

393
00:35:15,006 --> 00:35:23,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, nibbāna is not regarded as pleasant by default, because it's the cessation of emotions, it's the cessation of being.

394
00:35:24,233 --> 00:35:29,416
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as he said, the majority of people in the world are dependent on being.

395
00:35:29,436 --> 00:35:30,337
[SPEAKER_00]: They delight in being.

396
00:35:30,677 --> 00:35:35,001
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, that's why he didn't want to teach the dhamma in the first place, because he felt like nobody would understand.

397
00:35:35,942 --> 00:35:42,689
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could put it that way, because most people would not even be able to conceal the fact that not having emotions is good.

398
00:35:43,170 --> 00:35:54,163
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, you felt like, well, I'm going to talk to this, to these people, and it's just going to be... result in frustration, because, like, it's going against the entire thing that defines them.

399
00:35:56,304 --> 00:36:06,550
[SPEAKER_00]: Training yourself to see that going against the grain is pleasant, or is certainly better than what you have.

400
00:36:07,590 --> 00:36:10,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's not immediately...

401
00:36:11,432 --> 00:36:19,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if there is some dissonance at the beginning, you use your discernment to see like, well, no, it would be better not to be controlled by this.

402
00:36:21,829 --> 00:36:27,872
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, right now, I am suffering on account of this practice, because I'm being controlled by my emotions.

403
00:36:28,193 --> 00:36:31,955
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why dhamma practice is difficult, because the emotions are still there.

404
00:36:32,175 --> 00:36:32,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

405
00:36:32,635 --> 00:36:49,625
[SPEAKER_01]: So... Have you cleared up sufficiently the difference between emotion, feeling and sensation?

406
00:36:53,157 --> 00:36:54,091
[SPEAKER_02]: How do you feel about it?

